---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { states, stateRates, lastVerified } from '../../lib/data.ts';
import { formatDate, VERIFICATION_LABEL } from '../../lib/law.ts';
import { SITE, absoluteUrl } from '../../config.ts';

const byLevel = (level: string) => states.filter((s) => s.verification === level && (s.status === 'legal' || s.status === 'legal_soon')).length;
const openCount = states.reduce((n, s) => n + s.open_questions.length, 0);
const embed = `<iframe src="${absoluteUrl('/embed/', Astro.site)}" title="Is plug-in solar legal where I live?" width="100%" height="330" style="border:0;max-width:480px" loading="lazy"></iframe>`;
---
<Layout title="Method and data" description="How SolarWatt checks state laws, estimates solar production and savings, and how to correct or reuse the open dataset.">
  <section>
    <div class="narrow prose stack">
      <h1>Method and data</h1>
      <p class="lede muted">What this site knows, how it knows it, and where it could be wrong.</p>

      <h2>The law records</h2>
      <p>
        There is one file per state in <a href={`${SITE.repo}/tree/main/data/states`}>data/states</a>. Each one cites its
        sources, carries the date it was last checked ({formatDate(lastVerified)} at the moment), and is labelled
        with how well it has been verified:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {Object.entries(VERIFICATION_LABEL).map(([key, v]) => (
          <li><strong>{v.label}.</strong> {v.explain} <span class="muted">({byLevel(key)} of the states with a signed law)</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        Trackers and news stories disagree with each other more often than you would hope, mostly on effective dates.
        When they do, the record lists the disagreement under "Not yet settled" and the site shows the more cautious
        reading, such as the later start date. There are {openCount} open points right now. Closing them by reading
        the enacted text is the most useful thing a contributor can do.
      </p>
      <p>
        A signed law with a future start date flips to "legal" by itself on that date, in your browser, so the site
        does not go stale on January 1.
      </p>

      <h2>The savings estimate</h2>
      <p>
        Production comes from a model that runs in your browser. It takes about 20 years of monthly sunshine and
        temperature data from NASA's POWER project on a one-degree grid, splits each month into clear and overcast
        days, and works out how much light lands on a panel at your tilt and direction through each day, using the
        standard methods from solar engineering textbooks (Erbs, Collares-Pereira and Rabl, Hay and Davies). It
        then applies heat losses, 14% system losses, and inverter limits.
      </p>
      <p>
        Checked against NREL's PVWatts for tilted, south-facing systems, it lands within about 10% across cities
        from Seattle to Phoenix. The test suite enforces that. It does not know about the tree outside your window:
        shade is the biggest source of error, so be honest with the shade setting.
      </p>
      <p>
        Savings are not simply production times price. No plug-in solar law pays you for power sent to the grid, so
        the model compares the panel's output through the day against what your home is using and only counts what
        you use yourself. That is why the daytime-use setting moves the answer so much.
      </p>
      <p>
        Electricity prices are each utility's average residential price for {stateRates.year}, from NREL's
        utility-rates-by-ZIP dataset. They are a starting point. Your bill has the real number, and in
        deregulated states the dataset sometimes has only the delivery charge, in which case we fall back to the state average.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources of data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>ZIP code locations: US Census Bureau, 2024 ZCTA Gazetteer.</li>
        <li>Utilities and rates by ZIP: NREL and OpenEI, U.S. Electric Utility Companies and Rates ({stateRates.year}).</li>
        <li>Sunshine and temperature: NASA POWER climatology (CERES and MERRA-2).</li>
        <li>Law records: state legislature sites, plus the news and tracker sources cited on each state page.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Use the data</h2>
      <p>
        The law dataset is public domain (CC0). Take it: <a href="/data/states.json">JSON</a>,
        <a href="/data/states.csv">CSV</a>, or the <a href="/feed.xml">feed of changes</a>. A link back is appreciated but not required.
      </p>
      <p>To put the ZIP lookup on your own site, paste this:</p>
      <pre style="white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere;background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:1rem;font-size:.85rem"><code>{embed}</code></pre>

      <h2>Fix something</h2>
      <p>
        Every state page has an "Edit this record" link that opens the file on GitHub. Change the fact, add the
        source you read it in, update the date, and open a pull request. Automated checks make sure the record is
        well formed. If GitHub is not your thing, <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new/choose`}>open an issue</a> and
        describe what is wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site is not</h2>
      <p>
        It is not legal, electrical, or financial advice, and nobody here is a lawyer or an electrician. It is a
        careful summary with its sources showing. Before you plug anything in, read your state's law, check with
        your utility, and follow the manufacturer's instructions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
